    "lawCode": "Amd §7.17, Ment Hyg L",
    "lawSection": "Mental Hygiene Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S7274\n\nSPONSOR: PARKER\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the mental hygiene law, in relation to a program for the\ndiagnosis and treatment of post-traumatic stress disorder\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nAmends section 7.17 of the mental hygiene law to require the Office of\nMental Health (OMH) to develop a training program which includes a\ncomponent on military culture competency for mental health providers and\nclinicians to diagnose and treat veterans with post-traumatic stress\ndisorder.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIt is estimated that 11-20% of Veterans of the Iraq and Afghanistan wars\nand about 30 of Vietnam Veterans have PTSD. Many veterans' experience\nsituations which most civilians have never experienced.  These situ-\nations can be unfamiliar to mental health providers who may be tasked\nwith diagnosing and treating a veteran who has PTSD.\n\nWhen veterans return home with symptoms of PTSD, they are often referred\nto mental health professionals who may have no military experience and\ntherefore little to no knowledge of military culture.  Mental health\nproviders in rural areas have the additional challenge of, at times,\nbeing a great distance away from any training programs that may be able\nto help them in regards to the diagnosis and treatment of PTSD. This\nbill requires the program be placed on the website of OMB along with\nother resources the Office already offers.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2021-22: S5648- Passedthe Senate,Referred to Veterans Affairs\n\n2019-20: S5046 Committedto Rules\n\n2017-18: S5367 REFERREDTO MENTAL HEALTH AND DEVELOPMENTAL DISABILITIES\n\n2015-16: A1256 Referred to Veterans' Affairs\n\n2013-14: A7737 Referred to Veterans' Affairs\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nMinimal to state.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n",
